{
  "abstract": "We evaluate the complete low-character contribution to cubic Möbius–Gauss norms over the Eisenstein integers, including the exact polar-corrected squarefree norm. Splitting the exact transform by its effective cube-free core retains every low class as a positive sum of Euler-weighted inverse polynomials, while existing actual-Möbius estimates give a fixed power error for the remaining cores.\n\nA finite positive squarefree sieve produces a signed divisor kernel that remains positive through a product-measure representation. Recombining divisor overlaps yields an arithmetic truncation error $O(Y^{4/3+\\epsilon}/y)$, and the full polar cross comparison cancels the ordinary principal class. We then construct physical residue vectors for every retained divisor coordinate. Centered prime-divisibility indicators reproduce the entire positive model, including its daughter weights. Uniform Gram and adjoint estimates for a short growing family supply a matching physical lower bound. The complex adjoint pairings also reconstruct the actual output vector with a fixed power saving in squared error. Thus the exact corrected squarefree norm equals the explicit nonprincipal model with a fixed power error, and the finite sieve's physical excess also has a fixed power saving.\n\nA fixed-vector consequence shows that a common power saving below $Y^{4/3}$ for this norm for every fixed smooth complex profile is equivalent to a common zero-free half-plane for the specified nonprincipal cubic family. Neither condition is established. The result asserts no complete orthogonal basis, no new zero-free region and no Riemann-hypothesis conclusion. Finite phase, mask and local-moment checks support the algebra; the uniform bounds are analytic.",
